WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.107 --> 00:00:03.804
- Gary who's your
second in command?

00:00:03.804 --> 00:00:06.273
- At VaynerMedia today?
- Sure.

00:00:06.273 --> 00:00:07.808
- I would say
it's Claude Silver.

00:00:07.808 --> 00:00:09.910
She's our chief heart officer.

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:12.880
I think when you're, when you
have 700 employees and you're

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:17.117
selling people that's the asset
and so when you were talking

00:00:17.117 --> 00:00:18.652
about culture I was laughing
because you're like,

00:00:18.652 --> 00:00:19.720
"It's awesome," I'm like

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:21.622
"I wonder what the 
other 5000 people think."

00:00:21.622 --> 00:00:24.758
Right, because culture is
a one person thing.

00:00:24.758 --> 00:00:27.961
And so when I think about
culture, I'm sure you agree,

00:00:27.961 --> 00:00:31.331
I know that we're trying so so
hard, way, way more than the

00:00:31.331 --> 00:00:34.626
norm but I don't know 
what it means to all 700.

00:00:34.626 --> 00:00:37.337
We ask and 
we're counterpunchers.

00:00:37.337 --> 00:00:39.773
We don't push it.
We respond to it.

00:00:39.773 --> 00:00:44.044
And so at Vayner, it's Claude
Silver as chief heart officer.

00:00:44.044 --> 00:00:44.711
Then I would say it's

00:00:44.711 --> 00:00:48.181
James Orsini who's our 
chief operating officer.

00:00:48.181 --> 00:00:50.965
- What's a bigger business?
Wine Library or Vaynermedia?

00:00:50.965 --> 00:00:52.212
- VaynerMedia.

00:00:52.212 --> 00:00:54.265
- And so I watched you
grow that from 12 people.

00:00:54.265 --> 00:00:55.052
- Yep.

00:00:55.052 --> 00:00:56.487
- That was the first time
we kind of worked together.

00:00:56.487 --> 00:00:57.995
- That's right.

00:00:57.995 --> 00:00:59.066
- And I've kind of understood

00:00:59.066 --> 00:01:00.267
some of the pain 
you've gone through.

00:01:00.267 --> 00:01:02.729
What's been the
hardest thing about that?

00:01:02.729 --> 00:01:04.336
Actually, what I really 
want to say is

00:01:04.336 --> 00:01:06.336
you've operated smaller businesses,

00:01:06.336 --> 00:01:08.539
what's it like to actually run?

00:01:08.539 --> 00:01:10.844
When you walk into the new
space you're blown away.

00:01:10.844 --> 00:01:13.388
What is it like to
operate that kinds of business?

00:01:13.388 --> 00:01:14.885
- So far, so good.

00:01:14.885 --> 00:01:17.945
The hardest part and have what
it feels like is the same answer

00:01:17.945 --> 00:01:21.181
which is how do you
get buy-in at scale?

00:01:21.181 --> 00:01:23.817
It's a lot easier to get 30
people to buy in to you and your

00:01:23.817 --> 00:01:26.520
vision than it is 500 and 700.

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:29.623
That's fun for me to see four or
five of in them here and they're

00:01:29.623 --> 00:01:31.905
all in different parts of their
career at Vayner right now,

00:01:31.905 --> 00:01:34.161
in different
challenges or opportunities.

00:01:34.161 --> 00:01:37.631
- You ever have to think about
mapping people's careers before?

00:01:37.631 --> 00:01:38.966
- I've always
thought about that.

00:01:38.966 --> 00:01:41.702
I literally reverse
engineer every single person.

00:01:41.702 --> 00:01:43.904
I have context on how they came
from, the conversations we've

00:01:43.904 --> 00:01:46.740
had, who their mentors were
before, what they thought.

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:49.943
Emily's mom thought that
we were some mafioso thing.

00:01:49.943 --> 00:01:51.483
(audience laughter)

00:01:51.483 --> 00:01:53.718
I know who Sam's mentor was.

00:01:53.718 --> 00:01:55.085
I understand where Lisa worked

00:01:55.085 --> 00:01:56.943
in a traditional 
agency for 14 years.

00:01:56.943 --> 00:02:00.558
I know that Garrett went to
the same school as my children.

00:02:00.558 --> 00:02:01.463
I know shit.

00:02:01.463 --> 00:02:06.081
Like I am the head of
HR, CEO and so always.

00:02:06.081 --> 00:02:09.888
Wine Library, VaynerMedia and
everything I do forever it's

00:02:09.888 --> 00:02:12.466
only a people game
everything else continues to get

00:02:12.466 --> 00:02:13.867
commoditized by technology.

00:02:13.867 --> 00:02:15.235
This is a human thing.

00:02:15.235 --> 00:02:17.738
We are humans, we are
the ultimate species.

00:02:17.738 --> 00:02:22.607
We've won, it's us and 
we are the game and

00:02:22.607 --> 00:02:24.177
so yes I have 
thought about that.

00:02:24.177 --> 00:02:27.214
I think the reason we built one
of the fastest growing agencies

00:02:27.214 --> 00:02:31.752
ever is because of 
that part of it and

00:02:31.752 --> 00:02:33.720
the biggest challenge 
was getting buy-in.

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:38.025
Knowing that at 200 people there
were so many people that didn't

00:02:38.025 --> 00:02:42.462
believe me that thought tomorrow
I'm gonna do something bad or

00:02:42.462 --> 00:02:44.664
I'm doing this because I want
to buy the Jets and I don't care

00:02:44.664 --> 00:02:47.401
about them or
whatever it may be.

00:02:47.401 --> 00:02:49.569
It's been a lot of fun for
me because now we're a little

00:02:49.569 --> 00:02:52.873
further along and everybody
who's been waiting every day

00:02:52.873 --> 00:02:55.642
that it doesn't switch, it
doesn't turn, it doesn't become

00:02:55.642 --> 00:02:58.378
about the money they aren't
forced to do things, we keep

00:02:58.378 --> 00:03:01.815
listening you know it's
like gaining trust, right?

00:03:01.815 --> 00:03:05.686
And so now we've got, we are
much tighter and stronger at 700

00:03:05.686 --> 00:03:08.789
than we were at 250 which is
usually the other way but that's

00:03:08.789 --> 00:03:10.991
what happens when you
actually doubled down on it.

00:03:10.991 --> 00:03:13.727
And when the stories translate
and it's not always perfect.

00:03:13.727 --> 00:03:15.996
Even the people in this room,
it's not clicking perfectly

00:03:15.996 --> 00:03:18.065
right this second but
you put in the effort.

00:03:18.065 --> 00:03:18.732
You put in the effort.

00:03:18.732 --> 00:03:20.367
You play a forever game.

00:03:20.367 --> 00:03:23.170
You treat them just as well on
the way out and forever and help

00:03:23.170 --> 00:03:24.805
it's a people game.

00:03:24.805 --> 00:03:26.006
Yeah I've always thought that

00:03:26.006 --> 00:03:28.618
it's really the only 
move I've got.

00:03:28.618 --> 00:03:29.829
- I can attest to that.

00:03:29.829 --> 00:03:32.065
(hip hop music)

